<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Proclamation of the People's Republic of China</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as founding of the People's Republic of China</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> was proclaimed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a>, chairman of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a>, on 1 October 1949 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiananmen">Tiananmen</a> Square in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, a ceremony that also proclaimed the Central People's Government and marked the foundation of contemporary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. It came as the Communists won the final phase of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-civil-war">Chinese Civil War</a> against the Kuomintang-led Nationalist government of the Republic of China, which retreated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> by December 1949. At the ceremony the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-of-the-volunteers">March of the Volunteers</a> was played as the national anthem, the Five-starred Red Flag was raised for the first time to a 21-gun salute, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a> held its first public parade.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/proclamation-of-the-peoples-republic-of-china.jpg" alt="Proclamation of the People's Republic of China" width="330" height="242" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Orihara1 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mao_Proclaiming_New_China.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-civil-war">Chinese Civil War</a> between the Nationalist government and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> was fought intermittently from 1927. The party had earlier proclaimed a state of their own, the Chinese Soviet Republic, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruijin">Ruijin</a> on 7 November 1931, within the scattered territories they held; its government moved north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yanan">Yan'an</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-march">Long March</a>, and it was abolished in 1937. Hostilities paused from 1937 to 1945 while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-united-front">Second United Front</a> fought the Japanese invasion, and in the final phase, from 1945 to 1949, the party gained the upper hand.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> at first suggested keeping the name Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> under new leadership, but the overseas Chinese entrepreneur Tan Kah Kee objected and urged a clean break, and the committee accepted Zhang Xiruo's proposal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>. On 21 September 1949 Mao announced the new state in a speech to the first plenary session of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-peoples-political-consultative-conference">Chinese People's Political Consultative Conference</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>At 15:00 Beijing Time on 1 October, Mao spoke from the top of the Tiananmen Gate. His proclamation said that the conference had enacted the organic law of the Central People's Government, elected him its chairman, with Zhu De, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-shaoqi">Liu Shaoqi</a>, Song Qingling, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-jishen">Li Jishen</a>, Zhang Lan and Gao Gang as vice chairmen, and chosen Beijing as the capital. The government council adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/common-program">Common Program</a> as its policy, named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-enlai">Zhou Enlai</a> premier and foreign minister and Zhu De commander-in-chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a>, and declared itself the sole legal government of all the people.</p><p>The parade that followed, commanded by Nie Rongzhen and inspected by Zhu De, involved around 16,000 officers and personnel; no earlier Chinese government had held a public review of troops. Liu Bocheng, who had watched a parade on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-square">Red Square</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, proposed the Soviet format.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> recognised the new state on 2 October 1949, the first country to do so, and other communist states followed, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. Many Western countries, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> among them, continued to recognise the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>, and both governments claimed to be the legitimate government of all China. Chiang Kai-shek called the new government a puppet regime of the Soviet Union. The People's Republic moved quickly to consolidate its power and nationalise industries, and 1 October became its National Day.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Proclamation of the People's Republic of China” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
